<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-constant-angle-loci" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
    <title>Constant Angle Loci</title>
    <objectives component="outcomes">
        <introduction>
            <p>
            <dl>
                <li>
                    <title>Strand</title>
                    <p>
                        2.0 Measurements and Geometry
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <title>Sub-Strand</title>
                    <p>
                        2.3 Loci
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <title>Specific Learning Outcomes</title> 
                    <p>
                        Construct loci of points given constant angle in different situations
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li component="lesson-plan">
                    <title>Grade Level</title>
                    <p>
                        12
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li component="lesson-plan">
                    <title>Estimated Duration</title>
                    <p>
                        40 minutes
                    </p>
                </li>
            </dl>
            </p>
        </introduction>
    </objectives>

    <!-- Planning notes (from the Combined Mathematics tab of the Grade 12 Book Structure Mapping sheet)
         Content summary: Constant angle loci: points subtending a fixed angle on a line segment (arcs).
         Suggested activity: Learners place two goalposts and find points on the field from which the goal subtends the same angle, using a protractor and string.
         Interactive (JSXGraph): Fixed segment AB and a slider for angle θ; a point P moves so that ∠APB = θ, tracing the circular arcs. -->

    <!-- Section notes (2026-11)
         SLOs: Core 2.3 (e)
         Activity: as planning notes, in a classroom form | changed: a card angle sliding against two pins replaces the goalposts, protractor and string (kept as the outdoor version in the teacher's role and as the context of ex-school-goal-angle)
         JSXGraph: fig-constant-angle-locus (assets/jsxgraph/constant-angle-locus.js), as planning notes, with the centre and the mirror arc added
         STACK: assets/stack/top/2-measurement-and-geometry/08-loci/05-constant-angle-loci/ -->

    <p component="lesson-plan">Upon completion of this lesson, learners will be able to:
        <ol>
            <li><p><alert>Know:</alert> the points at which a segment <m>AB</m> subtends a fixed angle lie on arcs through <m>A</m> and <m>B</m>; the centre is on the perpendicular bisector of <m>AB</m>; the angle at the centre is <m>2\theta</m>; the angle in a semicircle is <m>90^{\circ}</m>; points on the other arc of the same circle see <m>AB</m> at <m>180^{\circ} - \theta</m>.</p></li>
            <li><p><alert>Do:</alert> construct the arc for a given angle; find its centre and radius; find a point at which <m>AB</m> subtends a right angle.</p></li>
            <li><p><alert>Apply:</alert> the angle a goal makes at a player.</p></li>
        </ol>
    </p>

    <axiom component="lesson-plan">
        <title>Problem-Solving and Discovery</title>
        <dl>
            <li><title>Suggested Time</title><p>14 minutes</p></li>
            <li><title>Objective</title><p>Learners slide a card angle against two pins and see that its corner traces an arc of a circle through the pins.</p></li>
            <li><title>Teacher's Role</title><p>Each pair needs a piece of card cut with a <m>50^{\circ}</m> corner (and one with a <m>90^{\circ}</m> corner), and two drawing pins pushed <m>6\,\text{cm}</m> apart through paper on a piece of cardboard. Outside, the same idea works with two goalposts: learners stand where a protractor held at eye level shows the goal at the same angle, and mark their places with string. After part (b), ask: <q>Where is the centre of the circle your points lie on?</q></p></li>
        </dl>
    </axiom>

    <activity xml:id="act-card-angle-between-two-pins">
        <title>A Card Angle Between Two Pins</title>
        <statement>
            <p>Two pins <m>A</m> and <m>B</m> are <m>6\,\text{cm}</m> apart. A card has a corner of <m>50^{\circ}</m>. Slide the card so that one edge of the corner always touches <m>A</m> and the other edge always touches <m>B</m>.</p>
            <p>
                <ol marker="(a)">
                    <li>
                        <p>Mark the position of the corner of the card in six different places.</p>
                        <solution component="lesson-plan"><p>The six marks are all on the same side of <m>AB</m> (until the card is turned over), and each one sees <m>AB</m> at <m>50^{\circ}</m>.</p></solution>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>Your marks lie on an arc. Find the centre <m>O</m> of the circle it belongs to by constructing the perpendicular bisectors of two chords joining your marks.</p>
                        <solution component="lesson-plan"><p>The two bisectors meet at <m>O</m>, which also lies on the perpendicular bisector of <m>AB</m>, on the same side as the marks.</p></solution>
                        <note component="lesson-plan"><p>This reuses the circumcentre construction from Grade 11: the centre of a circle is equidistant from every point on it.</p></note>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>Measure angle <m>AOB</m>. How does it compare with the angle of the card?</p>
                        <solution component="lesson-plan"><p>About <m>100^{\circ}</m>, twice <m>50^{\circ}</m>: the angle at the centre is twice the angle at the circumference.</p></solution>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>Measure the radius <m>OA</m>.</p>
                        <solution component="lesson-plan"><p>About <m>3.9\,\text{cm}</m>. (In triangle <m>OMA</m>, with <m>M</m> the midpoint of <m>AB</m>, <m>OA = 3 \div \sin 50^{\circ} = 3.92</m>.)</p></solution>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>Turn the card over and slide it on the other side of <m>AB</m>. What do you find?</p>
                        <solution component="lesson-plan"><p>A second arc, the mirror image of the first in <m>AB</m>.</p></solution>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>Repeat with the <m>90^{\circ}</m> card. What kind of arc do you get, and where is its centre?</p>
                        <solution component="lesson-plan"><p>A semicircle on <m>AB</m> as diameter, with centre the midpoint of <m>AB</m> (the angle in a semicircle).</p></solution>
                    </li>
                </ol>
            </p>
        </statement>
    </activity>

    <axiom component="lesson-plan">
        <title>Structured Instruction</title>
        <p>
            <dl>
                <li><title>Suggested Time</title><p>8 minutes</p></li>
                <li><title>Objective</title><p>To formalise the constant angle locus, its centre and radius, and the construction.</p></li>
            </dl>
        </p>
        <paragraphs>
            <title>Key Takeaways &amp; Teacher Connection</title>
            <ol>
                <li><p><alert>Connecting to Learner Work:</alert> the Grade 11 fact that angles in the same segment are equal, read backwards, says that the corner of the card stays on one arc.</p></li>
                <li><p>The centre is on the perpendicular bisector of <m>AB</m>, and angle <m>AOB = 2\theta</m>.</p></li>
                <li><p>The construction: at <m>A</m> draw a line making <m>90^{\circ} - \theta</m> with <m>AB</m> on <m>P</m>'s side (for <m>\theta \gt 90^{\circ}</m>, <m>\theta - 90^{\circ}</m> on the other side); it meets the perpendicular bisector at the centre.</p></li>
                <li><p>The <m>90^{\circ}</m> card gave a semicircle; an obtuse angle gives the minor arc, with the centre on the other side of <m>AB</m>.</p></li>
            </ol>
        </paragraphs>
    </axiom>

    <insight xml:id="insight-constant-angle-loci">
        <title>Constant Angle Loci</title>
        <p>
            Let <m>\theta</m> be an angle between <m>0^{\circ}</m> and <m>180^{\circ}</m>. The locus of a point <m>P</m> on one side of a segment <m>AB</m> such that <m>\angle APB = \theta</m> is an arc of a circle through <m>A</m> and <m>B</m>. We say that <m>AB</m> <term>subtends</term> the angle <m>\theta</m> at every point of the arc: the segment fills the same angle at every point of the arc.
        </p>
        <p>
            <ul>
                <li><p>The centre <m>O</m> is on the perpendicular bisector of <m>AB</m>.</p></li>
                <li><p>For <m>\theta \lt 90^{\circ}</m>, <m>O</m> is on the same side as <m>P</m> and <m>P</m> is on the major arc, with <m>\angle AOB = 2\theta</m>, <m>\angle OAB = 90^{\circ} - \theta</m> and radius <m>\dfrac{AB}{2\sin\theta}</m>.</p></li>
                <li><p>For <m>\theta = 90^{\circ}</m> the arc is a semicircle on <m>AB</m> as diameter, with centre the midpoint of <m>AB</m>.</p></li>
                <li><p>For <m>\theta \gt 90^{\circ}</m>, <m>O</m> is on the other side of <m>AB</m> and <m>P</m> is on the minor arc; the radius is still <m>\dfrac{AB}{2\sin\theta}</m>.</p></li>
                <li><p>Points on the other arc of the same circle see <m>AB</m> at <m>180^{\circ} - \theta</m>, because opposite angles of a cyclic quadrilateral add to <m>180^{\circ}</m>.</p></li>
                <li><p>If <m>P</m> may be on either side of <m>AB</m>, the locus is two arcs, mirror images of each other in <m>AB</m> (not including <m>A</m> and <m>B</m>).</p></li>
            </ul>
        </p>
        <p>
            To construct the arc for <m>\theta \lt 90^{\circ}</m>: construct the perpendicular bisector of <m>AB</m>; at <m>A</m>, on <m>P</m>'s side, draw a line making an angle of <m>90^{\circ} - \theta</m> with <m>AB</m>; it meets the bisector at <m>O</m>; draw the arc with centre <m>O</m> and radius <m>OA</m>. For <m>\theta \gt 90^{\circ}</m>, draw the line at <m>\theta - 90^{\circ}</m> on the other side of <m>AB</m>.
        </p>
    </insight>

    <figure xml:id="fig-constant-angle-construction">
        <caption>Constructing the locus of <m>P</m> with <m>\angle APB = 60^{\circ}</m> on one side of <m>AB</m>.</caption>
        <image width="60%">
            <shortdescription>Segment AB of 7 cm with its perpendicular bisector through the midpoint M. At A a line makes 30 degrees with AB and meets the bisector at the centre O, 2.0 cm above M. A solid arc with centre O and radius 4.0 cm runs from A over the top to B; P on it has angle APB of 60 degrees. The mirror arc below AB is dashed.</shortdescription>
            <latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.55]
%% --- SET ---
\tkzDefPoint(0,0){A} \tkzDefPoint(7,0){B}
%% --- CALCULATE ---
\tkzDefMidPoint(A,B) \tkzGetPoint{M}
\tkzDefPoint(3.5,2.0207){O} \tkzDefPoint(3.5,-2.0207){O'}
\tkzDefPoint(3.5,6.0622){P}
%% --- DRAW ---
\draw[gray, thin] (3.5,-6.5) -- (3.5,7);
\tkzDrawArc[thick](O,B)(A)
\tkzDrawArc[thick, dashed](O',A)(B)
\tkzDrawSegments(A,B A,O)
\tkzDrawSegments[thin](P,A P,B)
\tkzDrawPoints[size=3pt](A,B,M,O,P)
%% --- MARK ---
\tkzMarkRightAngle[size=0.4](B,M,O)
\tkzMarkAngle[size=1.2](B,A,O)
\tkzMarkAngle[size=1](A,P,B)
%% --- LABEL ---
\tkzLabelPoints[below left](A) \tkzLabelPoints[below right](B) \tkzLabelPoints[below right](M)
\tkzLabelPoint[right](O){$O$} \tkzLabelPoint[above](P){$P$}
\tkzLabelAngle[pos=1.8](B,A,O){$30^{\circ}$}
\tkzLabelAngle[pos=1.6](A,P,B){$60^{\circ}$}
\end{tikzpicture}
            </latex-image>
        </image>
    </figure>

    <figure xml:id="fig-constant-angle-locus">
        <caption>Drag <m>P</m> along the arc: the angle stays the same. Move the <m>\theta</m> slider. Tick the boxes to show the centre and the arc on the other side. Drag <m>Q</m> inside and outside the arc.</caption>
        <interactive xml:id="interactive-constant-angle-locus" platform="jsxgraph" width="90%" aspect="16:12" source="jsxgraph/constant-angle-locus.js">
            <slate xml:id="jsxgraph-constant-angle-locus" surface="jsxboard" aspect="16:12"/>
            <instructions>
                <p>
                    <ul>
                        <li><p>Set the <alert>θ</alert> slider to <m>50^{\circ}</m> and drag <m>P</m> along the arc. Does angle <m>APB</m> change?</p></li>
                        <li><p>Tick <alert>Show centre</alert>. Compare angle <m>AOB</m> with <m>\theta</m>.</p></li>
                        <li><p>Move <m>\theta</m> to <m>90^{\circ}</m>, then above <m>90^{\circ}</m>. Where is the centre now?</p></li>
                        <li><p>Drag <m>Q</m> inside the arc and then outside it. When is angle <m>AQB</m> larger than <m>\theta</m>?</p></li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </instructions>
        </interactive>
    </figure>

    <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/example-intro.ptx" />

    <example xml:id="example-locus-for-sixty-degrees">
        <title>The Locus for <m>60^{\circ}</m></title>
        <statement>
            <p><m>AB = 7\,\text{cm}</m>. Construct the locus of a point <m>P</m> on one side of <m>AB</m> such that <m>\angle APB = 60^{\circ}</m>. Find the distance of the centre from <m>AB</m> and the radius of the arc.</p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                <alert>Step 1:</alert> Construct the perpendicular bisector of <m>AB</m>, meeting it at <m>M</m>.
            </p>
            <p>
                <alert>Step 2:</alert> At <m>A</m>, on <m>P</m>'s side, draw a line making <m>90^{\circ} - 60^{\circ} = 30^{\circ}</m> with <m>AB</m>. It meets the bisector at the centre <m>O</m>.
            </p>
            <p>
                <alert>Step 3:</alert> Draw the arc with centre <m>O</m> through <m>A</m> and <m>B</m>, on <m>P</m>'s side (<xref ref="fig-constant-angle-construction"/>).
            </p>
            <p>
                In the right-angled triangle <m>OMA</m>, <m>AM = 3.5\,\text{cm}</m> and <m>\angle OAM = 30^{\circ}</m>:
                <md>OM = 3.5\tan 30^{\circ} = 2.02, \qquad OA = \frac{7}{2\sin 60^{\circ}} = 4.04</md>
                The centre is <m>2.0\,\text{cm}</m> from <m>AB</m> and the radius is <m>4.0\,\text{cm}</m>. Check: <m>\angle AOB = 180^{\circ} - 2 \times 30^{\circ} = 120^{\circ} = 2 \times 60^{\circ}</m>.
            </p>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <example xml:id="example-right-angle-at-p">
        <title>A Right Angle at <m>P</m></title>
        <statement>
            <p><m>AB = 9\,\text{cm}</m>. Describe the locus of a point <m>P</m> on one side of <m>AB</m> such that <m>\angle APB = 90^{\circ}</m>.</p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                The angle in a semicircle is <m>90^{\circ}</m>, so <m>P</m> lies on the semicircle with diameter <m>AB</m>: centre the midpoint <m>M</m> of <m>AB</m>, radius <m>4.5\,\text{cm}</m>, not including <m>A</m> and <m>B</m> (panel (a) of <xref ref="fig-semicircle-and-obtuse-arcs"/>). The construction rule agrees: the line at <m>A</m> makes <m>90^{\circ} - 90^{\circ} = 0^{\circ}</m> with <m>AB</m>, so it meets the bisector at <m>M</m> itself.
            </p>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <example xml:id="example-obtuse-constant-angle">
        <title>An Obtuse Angle</title>
        <statement>
            <p><m>AB = 6\,\text{cm}</m>. Construct the locus of a point <m>P</m> on one side of <m>AB</m> such that <m>\angle APB = 120^{\circ}</m>. What angle does <m>AB</m> subtend at points on the rest of the same circle?</p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                The angle is more than <m>90^{\circ}</m>, so the centre is on the other side of <m>AB</m>. At <m>A</m>, on the side away from <m>P</m>, draw a line making <m>120^{\circ} - 90^{\circ} = 30^{\circ}</m> with <m>AB</m>. It meets the perpendicular bisector at <m>O</m>, below <m>AB</m>. The radius is
                <md>OA = \frac{6}{2\sin 120^{\circ}} = 3.46</md>
                about <m>3.5\,\text{cm}</m>. <m>P</m> lies on the minor arc, on the far side of <m>AB</m> from <m>O</m> (panel (b) of <xref ref="fig-semicircle-and-obtuse-arcs"/>).
            </p>
            <p>
                A point <m>Q</m> on the major arc forms a cyclic quadrilateral <m>APBQ</m>, so <m>\angle AQB = 180^{\circ} - 120^{\circ} = 60^{\circ}</m>.
            </p>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <figure xml:id="fig-semicircle-and-obtuse-arcs">
        <caption>(a) The semicircle on <m>AB</m> where <m>\angle APB = 90^{\circ}</m>. (b) The minor arc where <m>\angle APB = 120^{\circ}</m>; on the major arc of the same circle the angle is <m>60^{\circ}</m>.</caption>
        <image width="90%">
            <shortdescription>Two panels. (a) AB of 9 cm with a semicircle on it, centre M the midpoint of AB, and a point P on the semicircle with a right angle marked at P. (b) AB of 6 cm; the centre O is below AB, 1.7 cm from its midpoint; a solid minor arc above AB has P on it with angle APB of 120 degrees; the rest of the circle is dashed below AB, with Q on it and angle AQB of 60 degrees.</shortdescription>
            <latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.5]
%% --- SET --- panel (a)
\tkzDefPoint(0,0){A} \tkzDefPoint(9,0){B} \tkzDefPoint(4.5,0){M}
%% --- CALCULATE ---
\tkzDefPoint(6.75,3.8971){P}
%% --- DRAW ---
\tkzDrawArc[thick](M,B)(A)
\tkzDrawSegments(A,B) \tkzDrawSegments[thin](P,A P,B)
\tkzDrawPoints[size=3pt](A,B,M,P)
%% --- MARK ---
\tkzMarkRightAngle[size=0.5](A,P,B)
%% --- LABEL ---
\tkzLabelPoints[below left](A) \tkzLabelPoints[below right](B) \tkzLabelPoints[below](M) \tkzLabelPoint[above right](P){$P$}
\node at (4.5,-2.2) {(a)};
\begin{scope}[xshift=12cm]
%% --- SET --- panel (b)
\tkzDefPoint(0,0){A2} \tkzDefPoint(6,0){B2}
%% --- CALCULATE ---
\tkzDefPoint(3,-1.7321){O2} \tkzDefPoint(3,1.7321){P2} \tkzDefPoint(3,-5.1962){Q2}
%% --- DRAW ---
\tkzDrawArc[thick](O2,B2)(A2)
\tkzDrawArc[dashed](O2,A2)(B2)
\tkzDrawSegments(A2,B2 A2,O2) \tkzDrawSegments[thin](P2,A2 P2,B2 Q2,A2 Q2,B2)
\tkzDrawPoints[size=3pt](A2,B2,O2,P2,Q2)
%% --- MARK ---
\tkzMarkAngle[size=0.55](A2,P2,B2)
\tkzMarkAngle[size=1](B2,Q2,A2)
\tkzMarkAngle[size=1.4](O2,A2,B2)
%% --- LABEL ---
\tkzLabelPoint[left](A2){$A$} \tkzLabelPoint[right](B2){$B$} \tkzLabelPoint[right](O2){$O$}
\tkzLabelPoint[above](P2){$P$} \tkzLabelPoint[below](Q2){$Q$}
\tkzLabelAngle[pos=1.0](A2,P2,B2){\scriptsize $120^{\circ}$}
\tkzLabelAngle[pos=1.7](B2,Q2,A2){$60^{\circ}$}
\tkzLabelAngle[pos=2.1](O2,A2,B2){$30^{\circ}$}
\node at (3,-6.6) {(b)};
\end{scope}
\end{tikzpicture}
            </latex-image>
        </image>
    </figure>

    <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/assessment-intro.ptx" />
    <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/checkpoint.ptx" />

    <p>Type decimals with a point, for example <c>12.6</c>. Give answers to the accuracy asked for. Type angles in degrees as numbers, without the degree sign, for example <c>74</c>.</p>

