    <exercises xml:id="exercises-constant-angle-loci">
        <exercise xml:id="ex-construct-45-degree-arc">
            <statement><p><m>AB = 8\,\text{cm}</m>. Construct the locus of a point <m>P</m> on one side of <m>AB</m> such that <m>\angle APB = 45^{\circ}</m>. Give angle <m>OAB</m>, angle <m>AOB</m> and the radius of the arc.</p></statement>
            <answer><p><m>\angle OAB = 45^{\circ}</m>, <m>\angle AOB = 90^{\circ}</m>, radius <m>5.7\,\text{cm}</m></p></answer>
            <solution><p><m>\angle OAB = 90^{\circ} - 45^{\circ} = 45^{\circ}</m> and <m>\angle AOB = 2 \times 45^{\circ} = 90^{\circ}</m>. The radius is <m>\dfrac{8}{2\sin 45^{\circ}} = 5.66\,\text{cm}</m>, about <m>5.7\,\text{cm}</m>.</p></solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise xml:id="ex-right-angle-locus">
            <statement><p><m>AB = 5\,\text{cm}</m>. Construct the locus of a point <m>P</m>, on either side of <m>AB</m>, such that <m>\angle APB = 90^{\circ}</m>.</p></statement>
            <answer><p>The circle with diameter <m>AB</m>: centre the midpoint of <m>AB</m>, radius <m>2.5\,\text{cm}</m>, without the points <m>A</m> and <m>B</m>.</p></answer>
        </exercise>
        <exercise xml:id="ex-school-goal-angle">
            <statement><p>The goalposts of a school football pitch are <m>7\,\text{m}</m> apart. Using <m>1\,\text{cm}</m> to represent <m>1\,\text{m}</m>, draw the locus of points on the pitch from which the goal subtends an angle of <m>30^{\circ}</m>. What is the radius of the arc on the ground?</p></statement>
            <answer><p><m>7\,\text{m}</m> (<m>7\,\text{cm}</m> on the drawing)</p></answer>
            <solution><p>At one post draw a line at <m>90^{\circ} - 30^{\circ} = 60^{\circ}</m> to the goal line, on the pitch side; it meets the perpendicular bisector of the goal at the centre. The radius is <m>\dfrac{7}{2\sin 30^{\circ}} = 7\,\text{m}</m>, so the centre is as far from each post as the posts are from each other: triangle <m>OAB</m> is equilateral.</p></solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise xml:id="ex-angle-from-the-radius">
            <statement><p>The arc of a constant angle locus through <m>A</m> and <m>B</m> has radius <m>5\,\text{cm}</m>, and <m>AB = 8\,\text{cm}</m>. Find the angle <m>AB</m> subtends at points on the major arc, and at points on the minor arc.</p></statement>
            <answer><p><m>53.1^{\circ}</m> on the major arc, <m>126.9^{\circ}</m> on the minor arc</p></answer>
            <solution><p>From <m>r = \dfrac{AB}{2\sin\theta}</m>, <m>\sin\theta = \dfrac{8}{2 \times 5} = 0.8</m>, so <m>\theta = 53.1^{\circ}</m>. Points on the minor arc see <m>180^{\circ} - 53.1^{\circ} = 126.9^{\circ}</m>.</p></solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise xml:id="ex-angles-of-the-same-circle">
            <statement><p>Points on the major arc <m>AB</m> of a circle with centre <m>O</m> see <m>AB</m> at <m>35^{\circ}</m>. What angle do points on the minor arc see, and what is angle <m>AOB</m>?</p></statement>
            <answer><p><m>145^{\circ}</m>; <m>\angle AOB = 70^{\circ}</m></p></answer>
        </exercise>
        <exercise xml:id="ex-radius-formula">
            <title>Challenge: The Radius Formula</title>
            <statement><p>Show that the radius of the constant angle arc for an angle <m>\theta \lt 90^{\circ}</m> is <m>\dfrac{AB}{2\sin\theta}</m>.</p></statement>
            <hint><p>Use triangle <m>OMA</m>, where <m>M</m> is the midpoint of <m>AB</m>, and find angle <m>AOM</m>.</p></hint>
            <answer><p><m>OA = \dfrac{AM}{\sin\theta} = \dfrac{AB}{2\sin\theta}</m></p></answer>
            <solution><p><m>\angle AOB = 2\theta</m> and <m>OM</m> bisects it (triangle <m>OAB</m> is isosceles), so <m>\angle AOM = \theta</m>. In the right-angled triangle <m>OMA</m>, <m>\sin\theta = \dfrac{AM}{OA}</m> with <m>AM = \dfrac{AB}{2}</m>, so <m>OA = \dfrac{AB}{2\sin\theta}</m>.</p></solution>
        </exercise>
    </exercises>

    <axiom component="lesson-plan">
        <title>Differentiation</title>
        <table xml:id="table-differentiation-constant-angle-loci">
            <title>Differentiation Strategies</title>
            <tabular halign="left" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
                <row header="yes" bottom="medium"><cell>Learner Group</cell><cell>Strategy and Learning Experience</cell></row>
                <row><cell>Struggling Learners (Support)</cell><cell>Start with the <m>90^{\circ}</m> card and <xref ref="example-right-angle-at-p"/> (the semicircle), then the <m>60^{\circ}</m> case of <xref ref="example-locus-for-sixty-degrees"/>. Allow a protractor for the <m>90^{\circ} - \theta</m> angle at <m>A</m>. Assign <xref ref="ex-right-angle-locus"/> and <xref ref="ex-construct-45-degree-arc"/>.</cell></row>
                <row><cell>On-Level Learners (Core)</cell><cell>Full activity and all examples. Assign <xref ref="ex-construct-45-degree-arc"/> to <xref ref="ex-angles-of-the-same-circle"/>.</cell></row>
                <row><cell>Advanced Learners (Challenge)</cell><cell>All exercises including <xref ref="ex-radius-formula"/>. Extension: for which angle <m>\theta</m> is the centre <m>O</m> on <m>AB</m> itself? (Answer: <m>\theta = 90^{\circ}</m>, when <m>O</m> is the midpoint of <m>AB</m>.)</cell></row>
            </tabular>
        </table>
    </axiom>

